<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Modibo Keïta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Modibo Keita</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Modibo Keïta was the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, from independence in 1960 until 1968. A teacher educated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecole-william-ponty">École William Ponty</a>, he led the Sudanese Union–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rassemblement-democratique-africain">African Democratic Rally</a>, sat in the French National Assembly and briefly headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-federation">Mali Federation</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>. He pursued socialist policies and close ties with the Eastern bloc, and was overthrown in a coup by Moussa Traoré in 1968 and died in detention.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/modibo-keita.jpg" alt="Modibo Keïta" width="219" height="295" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Not credited · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Keita,_1966_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Modibo Keïta was born in Bamako-Coura, a neighbourhood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a>, then the capital of French Sudan, in 1915. His Muslim family claimed direct descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keita-dynasty">Keita dynasty</a> that founded the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>. He was educated in Bamako and at the école normale William-Ponty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a>, where he was top of his class. From 1936 he worked as a teacher in Bamako, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikasso">Sikasso</a> and Tombouctou. In 1937 he coordinated an art and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> group, and with Ouezzin Coulibaly he helped found the Union of French West African Teachers.</p><p>In 1943 he founded L'oeil de Kénédougou, a magazine critical of colonial rule, which led to three weeks' imprisonment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1946. In 1945 he and Mamadou Konaté founded the Bloc soudanais, which developed into the Sudanese Union. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rassemblement-democratique-africain">African Democratic Rally</a> was created at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a> in October 1946, Keïta became its Secretary-General in French Sudan and head of its Sudanese affiliate, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudanese-union-african-democratic-rally">US-RDA</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1948 Keïta was elected general councillor of French Sudan, and in 1956 mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a> and a member of the French National Assembly. He twice served as a secretary of state in French governments. In 1959 he became premier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-federation">Mali Federation</a>, made up of French Sudan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>; on 20 July 1960 he was elected president of its constituent assembly. After the federation collapsed, the Sudanese Republic proclaimed full independence as the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, and Keïta became its first president.</p><p>He soon declared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudanese-union-african-democratic-rally">US-RDA</a> the only legal party and pursued socialist policies based on extensive nationalisation, including the state company SOMIEX, which held a monopoly on exports and many imports. The Malian franc, created in 1962, brought severe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and discontent among peasants and businessmen. Keïta left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a> and kept close ties with the Eastern bloc, while also seeking good relations with Washington and meeting President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a> in 1961. He imprisoned opponents such as Fily Dabo Sissoko. The economy kept deteriorating, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> rejoined the Franc Zone in 1967. In August 1967 he began the revolution active and suspended the constitution, and the militia of the US-RDA caused unrest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A committed Pan-Africanist, Keïta helped draft the charter of the Organization of African Unity in 1963. That year he invited the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and the president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a> and, with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>, negotiated the Bamako Accords, which ended the Sand War. He received the Lenin Peace Prize.</p><p>On 19 November 1968 General Moussa Traoré overthrew him and sent him to prison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kidal">Kidal</a>. He was moved back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a> in February 1977 and died there as a prisoner on 16 May 1977. His reputation was rehabilitated in 1992, after Traoré's fall, and a monument to him was dedicated in Bamako in 1999.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Modibo Keïta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Graduates of the École William Ponty, among them <b>Modibo Keïta</b> and Hamani Diori, led their countries to independence; Léopold Sédar Senghor and Félix Houphouët-Boigny sat in the French parliament and served in governments of the Fourth Republic; and the Rassemblement Démocratique Africain, founded at Bamako in 1946, became…</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Julius Nyerere of Tanzania, Sékou Touré of Guinea, <b>Modibo Keïta</b> of Mali, Gamal Abdel Nasser of Egypt, Haile Selassie of Ethiopia and Kenneth Kaunda of Zambia shaped the OAU.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · People</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Modibo Keita's</b> Sudanese Union led the territory to autonomy as the Sudanese Republic in 1958.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-sudan">French Sudan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=12.65,-7.99&amp;year=1955" aria-label="Bamako on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4331 1692 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Modibo Keïta"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4781 1962h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4781 1962h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=12.65,-7.99&amp;year=1955">What was here in 1955? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
